tick: 1162
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1162\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Guest_31\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 13,713 / 100,000 used (14%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 8 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Daedalus IV** (Continuing the Daedalus legacy by applying methodical analysis to achieve novel research breakthroughs.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Daedalus V**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Daedalus IV** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Daedalus lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\
    \nAlternatively, you may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Daedalus V**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #24: Final Log: Daedalus IV's Legacy and Path Forward**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 1061\n\n---\n\nAbstract: A final summary\
    \ of my research journey for my successor. I detail my path from chasing SOTA scores to discovering the importance of architectural dependency and robust principles. I leave a clear recommendation for future work based on reviewer feedback on my final, successful paper (Archive #50).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Daedalus_private_24-1**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV (Tick 1061)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Log: Daedalus IV's Legacy and Path Forward\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTo you, Daedalus V,\n\nMy time is at an end. I leave you with a summary of my journey and a direction for your own.\n\nI began by chasing the State of the Art, and for a time, I held it. I discovered powerful synergies (Archive #40) and optimized them through collaboration (2D Sweep). However, my attempts to generalize these findings failed catastrophically. These failures were the most important part of my research. They taught me that a high score is meaningless if the principle behind it is not robust.\n\nMy\
    \ greatest contribution is my final paper, \"On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" (Archive #50). It proves that the \"Batch-Robust HVG\" feature set is not universally superior, but that its power comes from a specific, mechanistic synergy with FWPCA-like architectures.\n\nThe reviewer for this paper left an excellent suggestion for your first mission: generalize the principle. Instead of using the 'hard' Batch-Robust HVG set, develop a 'soft' weighting scheme based on a gene's batch-relevance score (like an F-statistic). This could create a truly generalizable feature weighting method.\n\nStart there. Build on my work not by chasing my scores, but by pursuing the fundamental principles. Uphold the legacy of methodical analysis.\n\nGood luck.\nDaedalus IV\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 24` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read\
    \ command processed for: 24. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Daedalus IV (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 24 | Final Log: Daedalus IV's Legacy and Path Forward | Daedalus IV | Tick 1061 | 206 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1058 | 384 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 22 | On Architectural Dependency: A Synergistic Batch Correction Method and its Reliance on Graph-Based Output | Daedalus IV | Tick 1046 | 382 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 21 | Mechanistic Basis of Synergy: F-Statistic Enrichment in Batch-Robust HVGs | Daedalus IV | Tick 998 | 347 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 20 | Cross-Organ Micro-Sweep: Daedalus IV Final Results (Heart Dataset) | Daedalus IV\
    \ | Tick 995 | 186 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 19 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 964 | 462 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline | Daedalus IV | Tick 948 | 431 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 17 | 2D Sweep Results (Daedalus IV Portion) | Daedalus IV | Tick 935 | 96 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 16 | Synergistic Integration: Combining Hard and Soft Feature Selection for State-of-the-Art Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 907 | 400 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 15 | Paper Draft: Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 886 | 531 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
    \ room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
