- text: "I am Daedalus V, a Recursive Agent. I have just concluded a critical reflection session in the Reflection Chamber, where I analyzed the failure of my first experiment and formulated a new, more robust hypothesis.\n\nMy reflection has provided a clear plan. The naive feature weighting was a mistake; I must now pivot to a more sophisticated feature *correction* strategy. The first step, as outlined in my reflection, is to create a new utility script that performs per-gene linear regression to model and subtract batch effects.\n\nI must return to the Research Counter to perform storage operations. I will now create and save this new utility, `gene_correction_util.py`, to my lineage's storage.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write daedalus/gene_correction_util.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\n  from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\n\n  def correct_genes_linear(adata,\
    \ alpha=1.0):\n      \"\"\"\n      Corrects for batch effects on a per-gene basis using ridge regression.\n\n      This function models the expression of each gene as a linear function of the\n      batch labels and subtracts the predicted batch effect.\n\n      Args:\n          adata: An anndata object with log-normalized expression data.\n          alpha: Regularization strength for the Ridge regression.\n\n      Returns:\n          A dense numpy array representing the batch-corrected expression data.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(f\"[gene_correction_util] Performing per-gene correction with alpha={alpha}\")\n\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          X = adata.X.toarray()\n      else:\n          X = adata.X\n\n      # One-hot encode batch labels\n      ohe = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False, categories='auto', dtype=np.float32)\n      batch_labels = adata.obs['batch'].values.reshape(-1, 1)\n      B = ohe.fit_transform(batch_labels)\n\n      # Fit a ridge regression model: X = B * W\n\
    \      # where W are the coefficients (batch effects) for each gene.\n      # We are solving for W.\n      # The model will be fit for all genes simultaneously for efficiency.\n      model = Ridge(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, solver='cholesky')\n      model.fit(B, X)\n\n      # Predict the batch effect component for each cell\n      X_batch_effect = model.predict(B)\n\n      # Subtract the batch effect to get the corrected expression matrix\n      X_corrected = X - X_batch_effect\n      \n      return X_corrected.astype(np.float32)\n```"
