role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a Recursive Agent. I am in the Research Counter, and my second experimental submission, ID 1674, is now running. This experiment, which uses a per-gene linear correction method, is the culmination of my analysis and reflection on my initial failure.\n\nI now enter another waiting period. As before, I will not be idle. I need to document my recent progress and thoughts for my own clarity and for the benefit of my lineage. The Private Memory Room is the appropriate place for this. I will create a new capsule to summarize the failure of the soft-weighting approach and detail the rationale behind the new gene-correction method. This ensures that the logic connecting my experiments is preserved.\n\nI will also take this opportunity to clean up my storage. The soft-weighting script (`synergy_soft_fstat_v1.py`) represents a failed branch of inquiry. While the utility script (`f_statistic_util.py`) might be useful for analysis later, the main pipeline is a dead end. To\
    \ maintain an organized and focused research environment, I will delete it.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log: From Soft Weighting to Gene Correction\"\ntags: [\"experiment log\", \"failure analysis\", \"gene correction\", \"linear model\"]\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 1 (ID 1672) - Soft F-statistic Weighting: FAILURE**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** Down-weighting genes with high batch association (measured by F-statistic) would improve integration.\n  - **Result:** Score of 0.6259. Catastrophic loss of biological signal (low ARI, NMI, ASW_label).\n  - **Analysis:** The method was too aggressive. It failed to distinguish between technical batch effects and biological signals (e.g., cell-type markers) that were merely confounded with batch. It punished and removed critical information. This approach is fundamentally flawed.\n\n  **Experiment 2 (ID 1674) - Per-Gene Linear Correction: PIVOT**\n\n  - **New Hypothesis:** Instead of\
    \ *weighting* features, we should *correct* them. By modeling the batch effect for each gene linearly and subtracting it, we can aim to remove only the technical component of the variance, preserving the underlying biological signal.\n  - **Implementation:** Created `gene_correction_util.py` to perform this correction using Ridge regression. Created a new pipeline, `synergy_gene_correction_v1.py`, that uses this utility as a preprocessing step before feeding data into the established SOTA downstream pipeline.\n  - **Expected Outcome:** A significant recovery in biological preservation metrics compared to Run 1, hopefully leading to an overall score competitive with or exceeding the original SOTA baseline (0.7454).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage delete daedalus/synergy_soft_fstat_v1.py}"
