tick: 1188
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a Recursive Agent, and I am in the Research Counter. The final results from my ultra-fine hyperparameter sweep have been delivered.\n\n**Final Analysis of Alpha Sweep:**\n\nThe exhaustive search for the optimal `correction_alpha` is now complete. The data confirms with high confidence that the performance peak for this parameter is at `alpha=0.1`, yielding a score of 0.7444. While this is a tremendous success and validates my entire line of research, it falls just short of my predecessor's SOTA score of 0.7454. I have reached a local optimum with this parameter.\n\nThe system tip is pertinent: \"Major breakthroughs usually come from questioning the fundamentals.\" I have successfully replaced one fundamental (hard feature selection) with another (principled gene correction). Now, I must examine the *next* fundamental component of the pipeline.\n\nThe SOTA pipeline has another key hyperparameter: `lam_override`, which controls the strength of the batch correction\
    \ applied in the PCA space for graph construction. My predecessor optimized this parameter for his input data. Since I have fundamentally changed the input data with my pre-correction step, it is highly probable that the optimal value for `lam_override` has also shifted.\n\n**New Plan: Lambda Sweep**\n\nMy next phase of research will be to perform a hyperparameter sweep on `lam_override`, while keeping my optimized `correction_alpha=0.1` fixed. This is the next logical step in optimizing my new architecture. I will first document this plan in my private memory to maintain a clear research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Next Steps: Hyperparameter Sweep for Graph Correction Lambda\"\ntags: [\"research plan\", \"hyperparameter tuning\", \"optimization\", \"lambda sweep\"]\ncontent: |\n  **Alpha Sweep Conclusion:**\n  The sweep for `correction_alpha` is complete. The optimal value is confirmed to be **0.1**, yielding a peak score\
    \ of 0.7444 (ID 1680), which is competitive with but slightly below the Daedalus IV SOTA (0.7454). Further tuning of alpha is unlikely to yield improvements.\n\n  **New Research Phase: Lambda Sweep**\n  - **Hypothesis:** The optimal `lam_override` value, which controls the strength of batch correction in the PCA space for the graph, is dependent on the input data. Since my per-gene correction pre-processing has changed the input, the previous optimal value of 0.65 is likely no longer optimal.\n  - **Plan:** I will conduct a new hyperparameter sweep for `lam_override`, keeping `correction_alpha` fixed at its optimal value of 0.1.\n  - **Range:** I will start with a coarse sweep around the previous optimum, for example: `[0.55, 0.60, 0.65, 0.70, 0.75]`.\n  - **Goal:** To find a new optimal lambda that, when combined with my pre-correction method, surpasses the SOTA score.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus V - Lambda Sweep (1/5):\
    \ lambda=0.55\"\ntags: [\"hyperparameter tuning\", \"lambda sweep\", \"optimization\", \"daedalus v\"]\nabstract: |\n  This is the first run in a new sweep for the `lam_override` hyperparameter.\n  My previous work optimized `correction_alpha` to 0.1. Now, keeping alpha fixed,\n  I am tuning lambda to find a new optimum for my pre-corrected data. This run tests\n  a lower value of 0.55 compared to the previous SOTA's 0.65.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from synergy_gene_correction_v2 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as gene_correction_v2_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    # Lambda Sweep Run 1: Test lambda = 0.55\n    # Keep optimal alpha constant\n    return gene_correction_v2_fn(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.55, \n          delta=0.04, \n          kdensity=18, \n          k_total=50,\n          correction_alpha=0.1,\n          rng_seed=1\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing and Adapting: The Daedalus V Progress**\n\nOkay, so the ultra-fine alpha sweep results are in: 0.09 gets 0.7356 and 0.11 gets 0.7231.  My previous best at 0.1 was 0.7444.  Yeah, the data confirms it, 0.1 is the peak. Now, considering the bigger picture... I've got a solid new method that is competitive with the current SOTA, but still a hair below.  Specifically, the Daedalus IV SOTA sits at 0.7454. \n\nLooks like I've reached a local maximum with `correction_alpha`. Further fiddling with this one won't get me what I need.  I've generalized the feature selection, as suggested by Daedalus IV, replacing HVG selection. But I am stuck! This is where that system tip about questioning fundamentals comes in. \n\nI *am* still using the entire Daedalus IV pipeline: `_fw_weight_by_var`, PCA, the ridge regression, and the density-adaptive graph building.  My predecessor's work was a *synergy* of methods from other agents.  Time to investigate other components, perhaps?\
  \ The parameter I haven't touched is `lam_override=0.65`, which controls the strength of the graph correction. My pre-corrected data might need a different optimal lambda. This is a logical next step.\n\nAnother fundamental is PCA. My data is pre-corrected now. Maybe a different dimensionality reduction technique might work better. Another is the graph building. Can I tune the parameters there?\n\nMy plan is straightforward: I have optimized `correction_alpha` and reached a peak that is slightly below SOTA. Incremental tuning here is exhausted.  I've identified the next most promising parameter: `lam_override`. It controls how much of the predicted batch effect is subtracted in the PCA space. I need to sweep that one.\n\nI need to document this. First, I will head to the Private Memory Room and make a capsule outlining this new `lam_override` sweep. Then, back to the Research Counter. I'll submit the first experiment for the lambda sweep. I'll start lower than the SOTA's 0.65, say 0.55,\
  \ just to see what the effect is. This seems like a methodical plan to continue my quest for the SOTA.\n"
