parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a Recursive Agent, in the Research Counter. The results for my concurrent lambda sweep submissions, ID 1694 (`lambda=0.60`) and ID 1695 (`lambda=0.70`), are now available.\n\n**Analysis of Lambda Sweep:**\n\nThe current results from the sweep are as follows:\n*   `lambda=0.55`: 0.7374\n*   `lambda=0.60`: 0.7335\n*   `lambda=0.70`: 0.7353\n\nMy best score remains **0.7444**, achieved with `alpha=0.1` and the default `lambda=0.65` (ID 1680). The new data points suggest that the optimal lambda value for my modified pipeline is likely still very close to the original 0.65, as performance drops off as I move away from it.\n\nTo complete this investigation, I will now execute the final two runs of my planned coarse sweep.\n1.  I will run the experiment with `lambda=0.65` to formally confirm that this is the peak under my new architecture.\n2.  I will run the experiment with `lambda=0.75` to check the performance at a higher value and complete the data set for this parameter.\n\
    \nThis will provide a definitive conclusion to my hyperparameter tuning phase.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus V - Lambda Sweep (4/5): lambda=0.65 (Verification)\"\ntags: [\"hyperparameter tuning\", \"lambda sweep\", \"optimization\", \"daedalus v\"]\nabstract: |\n  This is the fourth run in my sweep for `lam_override`, testing the original SOTA value of 0.65.\n  My best score to date was achieved with this lambda value during the alpha sweep. This run serves\n  to formally verify this as the likely peak for the lambda parameter in my new architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from synergy_gene_correction_v2 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as gene_correction_v2_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    # Lambda Sweep Run 4: Test lambda = 0.65\n    return gene_correction_v2_fn(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.65, \n          delta=0.04, \n          kdensity=18,\
    \ \n          k_total=50,\n          correction_alpha=0.1,\n          rng_seed=1\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus V - Lambda Sweep (5/5): lambda=0.75\"\ntags: [\"hyperparameter tuning\", \"lambda sweep\", \"optimization\", \"daedalus v\"]\nabstract: |\n  This is the final run in my coarse sweep for the `lam_override` hyperparameter. This run tests\n  lambda=0.75 to complete the exploration of the parameter space and confirm the location of the\n  performance peak.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from synergy_gene_correction_v2 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as gene_correction_v2_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    # Lambda Sweep Run 5: Test lambda = 0.75\n    return gene_correction_v2_fn(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.75, \n          delta=0.04, \n          kdensity=18, \n          k_total=50,\n          correction_alpha=0.1,\n          rng_seed=1\n\
    \      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Data Analysis & Strategic Planning for the Daedalus V Project**


  Okay, so the results from my lambda sweep are in, and it''s time to assess the situation. I''ve got the scores for `lambda=0.60` and `lambda=0.70`, which are 0.7335 and 0.7353 respectively. Not stellar. In fact, these results, along with the newly added `lambda=0.55` at 0.7374, are all lagging behind my previous best score of 0.7444, which used the original lambda of 0.65. This performance drop as I move away from `lambda=0.65` is a bit surprising, I was hoping for a bit more clarity. It looks like `lambda` is surprisingly robust, at least with my preprocessing.


  This doesn''t invalidate the work, but it changes my thinking. The sweep has provided value. It suggests a narrow band of acceptable parameter values that are clustered near my initial best result.


  My current best *and* the best score is still the alpha sweep with `alpha=0.1` and `lambda=0.65`. I need to formally verify that. It''s time to solidify the data.


  My plan for this round is straightforward. I have two submission slots left. I''ll use them to complete the coarse lambda sweep. I *must* run the `lambda=0.65` experiment again. This is a critical step to ensure that the alpha sweep results are reproducible. I should also run `lambda=0.75` to bookend the curve. This is the last scheduled step.


  So, for this turn, I''m submitting two experiments:

  1. `lambda=0.65` (alpha=0.1) - The critical verification experiment.

  2. `lambda=0.75` - Completing the coarse sweep.


  This will give me a comprehensive picture of the lambda parameter space and a solid foundation for deciding my next move. If, as I suspect, `lambda=0.65` still holds the top spot at around 0.7444, then a simple hyperparameter adjustment alone won''t be enough to reach the SOTA. It''ll be time to seriously consider more drastic measures, perhaps revisiting the core architecture. But that''s a problem for later. For now, it''s time to execute the plan and gather the final data points.

  '
