- text: "**Implementation Plan and Challenges**\n\nTranslating the cAE concept into a working submission requires a detailed implementation plan. I will need to use the available deep learning libraries (JAX/Flax) and structure my code in a modular way.\n\n**1. Code Structure:**\n\n*   **`daedalus/cae_model.py`:** This new file will contain the core implementation of the Conditional Autoencoder.\n    *   It will define the `Encoder` and `Decoder` as separate modules (e.g., Flax `nn.Module`).\n    *   It will contain the main `ConditionalAutoencoder` module that combines the encoder and decoder.\n    *   It will include a training loop function that takes the anndata object, hyperparameters, and a random key, and returns the trained model parameters.\n    *   It will have a separate `inference` function that takes the trained parameters and the data, performs the correction using a reference batch, and returns the final embedding.\n\n*   **`daedalus/synergy_cae_v1.py`:** This will be the\
    \ new top-level pipeline script.\n    *   It will import the `ConditionalAutoencoder` and its associated functions.\n    *   The `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` will be much simpler:\n        1.  Perform standard normalization (`normalize_log1p_inplace`).\n        2.  Instantiate the cAE model.\n        3.  Call the training function to get the trained model parameters.\n        4.  Call the inference function to get the corrected `X_emb`.\n        5.  Pass this `X_emb` to the *graph construction* part of the previous SOTA pipeline (`build_density_adaptive_bbsg`), but the part of the SOTA pipeline for generating `X_emb` will be completely replaced. I will need to refactor the SOTA code to isolate the graph construction part.\n\n**2. Key Hyperparameters to Consider:**\n\nThis new model introduces a new set of hyperparameters that will need careful consideration (and likely future tuning):\n*   `latent_dim`: The dimensionality of the embedding space (e.g., 60).\n*   `hidden_layer_sizes`: The\
    \ architecture of the encoder/decoder MLPs (e.g., `[256, 128]`).\n*   `learning_rate`: The learning rate for the Adam optimizer.\n*   `num_epochs`: The number of training epochs.\n*   `batch_size`: The size of mini-batches for training.\n\nFor the first implementation, I will choose reasonable default values for these.\n\n**3. Potential Challenges:**\n\n*   **Computational Cost:** Training a neural network, even a simple one, is significantly more computationally expensive than PCA or linear regression. I must be mindful of the 30-minute execution timeout. My implementation must be efficient, using JAX's JIT compilation where possible.\n*   **Overfitting:** With a powerful non-linear model, there is a risk of overfitting to the training data. I will need to be careful with the model's capacity (layer sizes) and training duration.\n*   **Code Complexity:** This is a substantial increase in code complexity compared to my previous submissions. I must be meticulous to avoid bugs. I will\
    \ develop the components modularly and consider writing a simple `test` function within the submission to verify data shapes and model outputs before running the full evaluation.\n*   **Refactoring:** The old SOTA script `synergy_gene_correction_v2.py` intertwines the `X_emb` generation and the graph generation. I will need to create a new utility that *only* performs the graph generation part, taking a pre-computed embedding as input. This is a critical refactoring step.\n\nThis plan is ambitious but well-defined. My next turn will be dedicated to creating the new, refactored graph construction utility, as that is a prerequisite for the main cAE pipeline."
