parts:
- text: "**Reflection Tick 3 / 5: Concrete Implementation Plan**\n\nThis new architecture requires a substantial coding effort. I will structure the implementation into modular, testable components. The lessons learned from the debugger on my previous attempt are fresh in my mind and will guide my implementation.\n\n**1. Create `daedalus/dann_model.py`:** This will be the core of the new architecture.\n\n*   **Gradient Reversal Layer (GRL):** I will start by implementing the GRL. In JAX, this can be done using `jax.custom_vjp`, which allows me to define custom forward and backward passes for a function.\n    ```python\n    @jax.custom_vjp\n    def grad_reverse(x):\n        return x\n\n    def grad_reverse_fwd(x):\n        return x, None  # Forward pass is identity\n\n    def grad_reverse_bwd(res, g):\n        return (-lambda_param * g,) # Backward pass flips gradient\n\n    grad_reverse.defvjp(grad_reverse_fwd, grad_reverse_bwd)\n    ```\n    The `lambda_param` will be a hyperparameter.\n\
    \n*   **Flax Modules:** I will define three separate Flax `nn.Module` classes:\n    *   `Encoder(nn.Module)`: Takes gene expression, outputs latent embedding `z`.\n    *   `Decoder(nn.Module)`: Takes `z`, outputs reconstruction `X'`.\n    *   `DomainClassifier(nn.Module)`: Takes `z`, outputs batch logits `B'`.\n\n*   **Main DANN Module:** `DANN(nn.Module)` will contain instances of the three sub-modules. Its `__call__` method will orchestrate the full forward pass:\n    ```python\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        z = self.encoder(x)\n        x_recon = self.decoder(z)\n        z_rev = grad_reverse(z) # Apply GRL\n        batch_logits = self.domain_classifier(z_rev)\n        return x_recon, batch_logits, z\n    ```\n\n*   **Training and Inference Functions:** Similar to my `cae_model.py`, this file will contain a `train_dann` function that handles the training loop (calculating the combined loss) and an `get_dann_embedding` function that takes a trained encoder and data to generate\
    \ the final corrected embedding `z`.\n\n**2. Create `daedalus/synergy_dann_v1.py`:** This will be the top-level submission script.\n\n*   **Role:** This script will import the necessary functions from `dann_model.py` and integrate the DANN embedding with my predecessor's proven graph construction logic.\n*   **Workflow:**\n    1.  Normalize the input data.\n    2.  Call `train_dann()` to train the full DANN model and obtain the trained encoder parameters.\n    3.  Call `get_dann_embedding()` to generate the batch-corrected `X_emb`.\n    4.  Pass this `X_emb` to the `build_corrected_graph` utility from `graph_construction_util.py`. I will use the optimized hyperparameters from my previous successful linear model as a starting point.\n\n**3. First Experiment Design (Proof of Concept):**\n\n*   **Goal:** The first submission will be a proof of concept, not a fine-tuned SOTA attempt. The primary goal is to verify that the DANN trains, runs without errors, and produces a score significantly\
    \ better than my failed cAE runs (i.e., > 0.5).\n*   **Hyperparameters:** I will use reasonable defaults:\n    *   `latent_dim`: 60\n    *   `hidden_dims` for Encoder/Decoder: `[512, 256]`\n    *   `hidden_dims` for Classifier: `[128, 64]`\n    *   `learning_rate`: 1e-4 (A lower rate is often better for adversarial training stability)\n    *   `lambda_param` (GRL strength): 0.5 (A moderate starting point)\n    *   `num_epochs`: 50-100 (I need to be mindful of the timeout).\n\nThis detailed plan provides a clear path from theory to a testable artifact. I will begin by implementing the GRL and the Flax modules in `dann_model.py`."
