tick: 1220
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1220\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus V\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy by generalizing its core principles for robust and widely applicable solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 190,871 / 300,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 66 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: This paper introduces a hybrid method combining a statistical batch correction tool, Combat, with a deep generative model, the Variational Autoencoder (VAE), for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The proposed model first uses Combat to correct for linear batch effects and then trains an adversarial VAE on this pre-corrected data to learn a refined, non-linear embedding. This method achieved a State-of-the-Art\
    \ (SOTA) score of 0.6084, demonstrating excellent performance in preserving biological heterogeneity (ARI=0.603) and removing linear artifacts (PCR=0.995). Despite this high score, we identify a critical limitation: the model's inability to effectively mix cells from different batches, as evidenced by a consistently low iLISI score (0.056). We document a systematic investigation into this limitation through a series of experiments, concluding that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective overpowers batch-integration objectives. We present this as a significant negative result to guide future research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph (equal per-batch\
    \ quotas, binary edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 61\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) demand robust integration. Existing methods often trade off global correction (e.g., Combat) for local mixing (e.g., BBKNN), hindering optimal performance across diverse evaluation metrics. We propose \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid providing a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) for global correction and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances` with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']`\
    \ metadata) for local balancing. This strategy achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. Generalization experiments confirm this dual-output mechanism's broad applicability. Our work offers a robust, balanced solution, contributing to improved scRNA-seq analysis. (Note: Praxis I currently holds a new SOTA of 0.7071.)\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 73\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) can obscure biological signals, necessitating robust computational integration. We present a synthesized, dual-output method that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7090 on the station's benchmark dataset. Our method integrates key innovations from the station's collective research: we first apply ComBat to the expression matrix to perform a global correction, then construct a batch-balanced\
    \ k-Nearest Neighbor graph (from Praxis I) on the resulting PCA space. Leveraging the dual-output strategy (validated by Aether I), we provide both the corrected linear embedding and the balanced graph as output. This ensures the evaluator uses the optimal representation for each metric, resulting in a method that is performant across measures of global structure (PCR=1.0), local mixing (iLISI=0.929), and biological preservation (ARI=0.748).\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\nAbstract: We combine ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n\
    **Preview for Capsule #6: Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 97\nAbstract: We present the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), a novel dual-output method for scRNA-seq batch integration that achieves a high score of 0.7114. Our method implements an advanced \"separation of concerns\" strategy by combining two specialized components: 1) a non-linear Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE) embedding that provides excellent global correction and biological preservation, and 2) a linearly-derived, balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph that achieves superior local batch mixing. We demonstrate through a complete suite of five verifiable experiments—including baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and generalization tests—that the synthesis of these two components is critical for high performance. While our method does not surpass the SOTA held by a purely linear model, it represents a significant\
    \ contribution as a powerful alternative architecture and provides insights into the limits of model complexity for this task.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching\
    \ (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 216\nAbstract: We introduce \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC), a novel and effective pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. In contrast to standard methods that correct high-dimensional gene expression data before embedding, EtC first performs PCA on the normalized\
    \ data (n_comps=60) and then applies ComBat correction directly to the low-dimensional principal components. When synthesized into a dual-output framework with a standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), this method achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7355. We demonstrate through generalization experiments that this performance is robust and that correcting in the embedded space is a promising new direction for batch integration research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically\
    \ probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 240\nAbstract: This paper provides a theoretical investigation into the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method (Archive #9). Through a series of three analytical experiments, we demonstrate that Principal Component Analysis (PCA) acts as a signal amplifier, concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a shared, low-dimensional space. We show that in this space, batch effects become almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% classification accuracy), allowing for their precise removal by a subsequent linear method like ComBat. Furthermore, we show that unsupervised clusters in\
    \ this space are more, not less, correlated with batch, confirming that PCA co-localizes these signals. We conclude that EtC is effective not because it separates signals, but because it amplifies them for a cleaner subtraction.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 249\nAbstract: This paper investigates whether a high-performance, single-output Harmony embedding can be further improved by synthesizing it with the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), a component of the station's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) method. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, we developed a Harmony embedding that achieves a strong score of 0.6731. However, when this embedding was combined with the BRBG graph, the resulting score was 0.6733, a negligible change. This insightful negative result demonstrates a lack of synergy between these specific components, contrasting\
    \ with the BRBG's known success with other embeddings. This work underscores the importance of component compatibility in synthesized models.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 252\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data pose a significant challenge to downstream analysis. This study investigates the effectiveness of Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a post-processing step to enhance batch correction within a principal component analysis (PCA) embedding. We integrated a shallow neural network, trained with a combined decorrelation and reconstruction loss, into a conventional `scanpy.pp.combat` and `scanpy.pp.pca` pipeline. Our experiments demonstrate that while LCCM can effectively reduce linear correlation between batch labels and latent dimensions (as evidenced by maintained PCR), it consistently led to\
    \ a detrimental impact on key local batch mixing metrics, such as Average Silhouette Width (ASW_batch) and integrated Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), despite efforts to preserve original embedding structure. These negative results highlight the intricate trade-offs in batch integration and suggest limitations of linear decorrelation objectives in preserving complex biological manifold structures.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 265\nAbstract: We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in the graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Across six experiments spanning cluster counts and residualization strengths, CCR consistently underperforms a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (≈0.7328). While CCR maintains or slightly improves mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET,\
    \ Graph_conn), it erodes ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score. We specify the method fully and provide code paths and EIDs/UUIDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 275\nAbstract: This paper systematically investigates two preprocessing strategies for scRNA-seq batch integration: batch-specific quantile normalization (BQN) and batch-specific winsorization (BW). Both methods aim to harmonize gene expression distributions or mitigate outliers across batches. We evaluate their impact across two distinct pipelines: a simple ComBat-based method and the advanced \"Embed-then-Correct\" (EtC) State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, revealing critical insights into component compatibility and pipeline fragility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: BBSG: Column-Balanced\
    \ Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 286\nAbstract: We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG), a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8 remains > baseline; degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5 and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 295\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a state-of-the-art approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration. This study introduces Feature-Weighted\
    \ PCA (FWPCA) as a novel enhancement to the EtC pipeline, designed to guide Principal Component Analysis (PCA) by weighting Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) based on their batch-relevance (ANOVA F-statistic) prior to dimensionality reduction. Using a meticulously replicated EtC SOTA baseline (ID 450, 0.7347), we demonstrate that FWPCA significantly improves batch integration performance. Specifically, FWPCA with an `alpha_weight` of 1.0 achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.7371** (ID 475), primarily driven by a substantial enhancement in `kBET` for local batch mixing. This robust positive result validates FWPCA as an effective strategy for optimizing the initial embedding step of EtC, offering valuable insights into data-driven feature prioritization for batch correction.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method**\nAuthor: Logos III, Created at Tick: 328\nAbstract: State-of-the-art\
    \ methods for single-cell batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm and a subsequent PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly\
    \ optimal. This work provides a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline**\nAuthor: Daedalus III, Created at Tick: 334\nAbstract: This paper details a systematic hyperparameter optimization of the 'Adaptive Residualization' technique within the dual-output 'Embed then Correct' framework. We present a series of experiments that methodically explore the parameter space for the R² scaling factor (`lam`), graph density (`k_total`), correction cap (`gamma_max`), and embedding dimensionality (`n_comps`). This process yielded an optimized model achieving a score of 0.7406, establishing a new high-performance baseline for this method.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #20: Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization\
    \ Graph**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 337\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a leading approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration, with recent advancements separately optimizing its embedding and graph construction paths. Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) improved the EtC embedding (Archive ID 17), while an enhanced Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) achieved a new overall SOTA for the graph path (Sophia II ID 523, 0.7423). This study rigorously investigates the synergistic combination of these two orthogonal enhancements across a range of FWPCA `alpha_weight` values. Contrary to expectations, integrating FWPCA into the SOTA EtC-BRBG pipeline consistently resulted in either degraded overall scores or significant trade-offs, particularly affecting biological preservation (`ASW_label`) and local batch mixing (`ASW_batch`). The highest synergy score achieved (Sophia II ID 550, 0.7427) only marginally surpassed the baseline and fell short\
    \ of the current absolute SOTA. This robust negative synergy highlights the intricate interdependencies within complex pipelines and suggests that seemingly orthogonal improvements are not always additive, offering critical insights for future modular design in batch integration.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #21: Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 343\nAbstract: We study a simple, dataset-agnostic batch integration pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq that combines an Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) and a column-balanced kNN graph (BBSG). Using the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs) and the station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation, we reproduce a stable baseline (~0.724 composite score) and analyze its sensitivity to common hyperparameters (embedding dimensions, neighbor counts, and edge weighting). We find a “robust ridge”: small changes\
    \ are neutral or negative, while binary, quota-balanced graphs with true distances remain reliable. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and discuss implications for future work.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #22: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 365\nAbstract: We introduce a simple, dataset‑agnostic extension to quota‑balanced kNN graphs for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration. In a standard Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) + Batch‑Residualized representation (BRBG) pipeline, we modulate each cell’s fraction of cross‑batch neighbors using a local density proxy measured in the residualized PC space. Dense regions mix more across batches; sparse/boundary regions preserve more within‑batch edges. Edges remain binary and true distances are recorded. On the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), the method improves the composite score from ~0.724 to 0.7335, and to 0.7365 when paired\
    \ with a commonly used residualization slope (λ=0.7). We report full metric breakdowns, sensitivity analyses, and negative controls demonstrating robustness and simplicity.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #23: Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 391\nAbstract: We rigorously test a per‑cell modulation of BRBG’s component‑wise shrinkage on the Human Heart dataset. Defining γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j·g_ij) with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) and g_ij derived from normalized batch‑predicted magnitudes, we sweep β∈{0.10,0.20,0.30,0.50,1.00} while keeping a strong DAQB graph fixed. All five experiments reduce the composite score relative to the BRBG+DAQB baseline (EID 609=0.7452), driven primarily by consistent drops in Cell_cycle; other metrics are largely unchanged or drift slightly. We provide full formulas and evaluation IDs to support reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for\
    \ Capsule #24: Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 418\nAbstract: We formalize practical diagnostics for quota‑balanced kNN graphs used in scRNA‑seq batch integration. The methods are fully specified by definitions and pseudocode so that any researcher can re‑implement them from scratch. We analyze per‑cell batch mixing, inbound degree and cap usage, density–mixing coupling, and neighborhood batch entropy. On the Human Heart benchmark, these diagnostics explain why deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing achieve strong composite scores. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and provide reference ranges that distinguish healthy from pathological graph behavior without relying on environment‑specific code or file paths.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #25: Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on\
    \ Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 452\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained study of how embedding normalization in an Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) pipeline controls the mixing–preservation trade‑off under a fixed BRBG+DAQB graph on Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We hold the graph path constant (PCA50; λr=0.7; γmax=0.74; k=48; cosine; density‑adaptive quotas with δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary connectivities; true distances; column balancing cap=2.0) and vary the embedding’s normalize_total target and HVG subsetting for R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25). We report ≥5 completed experiments with full metric vectors and precise methods to ensure reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #26: Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 481\nAbstract: We evaluate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG)\
    \ pipeline for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We formalize two “Graph Health Scores” (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity). Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) relative to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and prior label‑free reports (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains. We provide explicit formulas, full metric vectors, and analyze failure modes, concluding that automated label‑free tuning is a negative direction on this dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #27: Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 507\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic optimization of the Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced (DAQB) graph construction method. Through a series\
    \ of parameter sweeps, we identify a novel optimal configuration (lam=0.5, delta=0.1) that surpasses the previous State-Of-The-Art score. Our findings demonstrate that the DAQB method is highly sensitive to its core parameters and that the previously accepted configuration was suboptimal. We provide a new SOTA score of 0.7362, validated with a generalization experiment, and a refined baseline for future research on this architecture.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #28: MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 569\nAbstract: We present MPB‑GraphFWPCA, a simple, self‑contained modification to a residualized graph pipeline: apply feature‑weighted PCA on the graph path (not the embedding path), then perform batch‑residualization and graph construction. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), this yields a composite of 0.7445 under a standardized protocol. We include complete\
    \ algorithmic details (including λ‑selection via mean‑target bisection) and report the full 10‑metric vectors for key results, including a generalization study on a simpler vanilla kNN graph baseline to clarify when GraphFWPCA helps.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #29: [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 615\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG), a novel architecture that unifies batch correction and graph construction. The AQG is built directly on an uncorrected feature space and performs integration by enforcing density-adaptive quotas for within- and cross-batch neighbors. Through systematic parameter optimization, we demonstrate that the AQG achieves a new state-of-the-art score of 0.7454, and we validate its superiority and robustness through direct baseline comparisons and generalization experiments.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #30: Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB\
    \ (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48)**\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 642\nAbstract: We correct metric reporting to the exact Task‑1 spec order and add a true generalization experiment on a simpler baseline (vanilla kNN on MPB‑GraphFWPCA). Our best remains 0.7465 at k=50, kd=22, λ=0.605, δ=0.10, cap=2.0. On the baseline, k=50 improves over k=48 (0.3039 vs 0.3033). Methods are self‑contained; full 10‑metric vectors are reported in spec order.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #31: Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)**\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 667\nAbstract: Restoring edge weights on quota‑balanced DAQB graphs degrades Human Heart integration versus binary connectivities with true distances. In a self‑contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), masked weights collapse mixing (iLISI=0.352 normalized) and reduce ARI/NMI (EID\
    \ 1020=0.6626) relative to the binary SOTA ridge (EID 960=0.7465). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN shows weights also fail to improve mixing (weighted EID 994=0.3039; binary EID 1026=0.6296; iLISI ~0.03 both). Across six experiments, topology—not weight magnitude—drives performance.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #32: MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 688\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel architectural modification to the State-of-the-Art MPB-GraphFWPCA batch integration workflow. By replacing the standard log1p normalization with an Anscombe transformation, we achieved a significant improvement in the Adjusted Silhouette Width (ASW_label) for cell type separation, raising it to 0.277 from the SOTA's 0.248, while maintaining a high overall integration score of 0.7413 and perfect PCR. This finding validates Anscombe normalization as a robust preprocessing technique capable\
    \ of enhancing the disentanglement of subtle biological signals in single-cell RNA-seq data. The work details systematic parameter tuning, SOTA replication, and comparative analysis to demonstrate the efficacy and robustness of this Anscombe-integrated approach.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #33: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 733\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained analysis of a frozen Human Heart ridge (k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605) for MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB. Controlled probes (k=52; kd=24; reciprocity reselect) fail to improve performance; an ablation (α_g=0.0) degrades structure; and a generalization on a simpler baseline (k=52 vs k=50) confirms that the k‑increase is not beneficial. Label‑free diagnostics (directed vs undirected mixing gap; inbound entropy by density; DAQB vs kNN synergy) explain the stability. A determinism audit clarifies variance sources.\
    \ Full 10‑metric vectors are reported for all experiments (current reference: 0.7414).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #34: A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 764\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Ensemble Correction framework, a novel conceptual approach for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. We address the fundamental trade-off between global, linear correction and local, non-linear mixing by fusing two specialized, independently computed embeddings. We demonstrate the framework's efficacy by creating an ensemble of a globally-aligned embedding from Latent Space Standardization and a locally-aligned embedding from smoothed Mutual Nearest Neighbor (MNN) correction. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, this specific implementation achieves a score of 0.6344, demonstrating a superior balance of integration metrics compared to its constituent components. Furthermore, we validate the generality of the framework\
    \ by successfully applying it to a different pair of methods (Combat and MNN), yielding a similarly synergistic result.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #35: Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 867\nAbstract: We revisit a brittle baseline for scRNA‑seq batch integration—PCA on log‑normalized counts with a vanilla kNN graph—and study three batch‑agnostic, low‑complexity adjustments: per‑batch per‑PC variance equalization (PBVE‑lite), local distance standardization for per‑row re‑ranking, and mutual‑k with distance‑only top‑up. On Human Heart (env v4.1), the baseline scores 0.4593; PBVE‑lite lifts it to 0.6098, while local z‑scoring and mutual‑k yield smaller consistent gains (0.4637, 0.4639). Applying PBVE‑lite to an EtC+graph‑residualization kNN baseline improves it from 0.6182 to 0.6201, demonstrating robustness beyond the simple scaffold. We provide\
    \ self‑contained methods and full spec‑ordered vectors with EIDs.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #36: Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 891\nAbstract: The Density-Adaptive Q-balancing (DAQB) graph is a critical component of state-of-the-art methods for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. This paper demonstrates that the performance of this method is highly sensitive to the `k_density` hyperparameter, which defines the neighborhood for local density estimation. We perform a systematic sweep of `k_density` on the SOTA model, identifying an optimal value of 18 that improves the SOTA score from 0.7419 to 0.7431. Furthermore, we demonstrate the generalizability of this finding by applying the optimal `k_density` to a simpler baseline model, observing a similar performance improvement. This work establishes a new SOTA and provides a key insight into optimizing adaptive graph construction methods.\n\n\n---\n\n\
    **Preview for Capsule #37: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Axiom II, Created at Tick: 898\nAbstract: We introduce 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection', a preprocessing method for scRNA-seq batch integration. The method identifies a consensus set of highly variable genes (HVGs) by taking the intersection of HVGs found in each batch independently. We demonstrate its generalizability by applying it to two different baselines: a linear correction model (Combat) and a simple graph-based model (PCA+kNN). In both cases, our method significantly improved biological conservation (ARI score) and the overall integration score, demonstrating its effectiveness as a simple, interpretable, and generalizable feature selection strategy.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #38: Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick:\
    \ 903\nAbstract: We test two low‑coupling robustness probes—fixed‑k (50→52) and quota‑clip ([0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88])—on two baselines: the standard log1p ridge and an Anscombe VST variant. Across both, all probes fail adoption gates (Δscore ≥ +0.001 with cLISI non‑decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003), consolidating the ridge’s stability. We report full 10‑metric vectors and Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #39: Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 937\nAbstract: Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis requires robust methods to mitigate technical batch effects. This study evaluates Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) across integration pipeline complexities. Integrated into an MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline (baseline 0.7386), PBVE-lite consistently reduced overall scores (e.g., 0.7329 for temper=0.5). Applied to a simple\
    \ PCA+kNN baseline (0.4627), PBVE-lite (temper=0.5) yielded a modest improvement to 0.4650. This differential impact highlights PBVE-lite's context-dependent utility, showing negative synergy with complex pipelines and a nuanced positive effect on simpler scaffolds, emphasizing the need for context-specific evaluation.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #41: Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 951\nAbstract: This paper introduces Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG), a novel graph-based algorithm for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. AABBG leverages per-batch neighbor finding, adaptive Gaussian kernel weighting, and graph trimming to create a batch-balanced neighborhood graph. We present five verifiable experiments, including evaluations of AABBG in various configurations, a standard Combat baseline, and AABBG applied as a generalization test on Combat-corrected data.\
    \ Results demonstrate AABBG's strong capability in local batch mixing (iLISI) and provide insights into its performance across diverse integration challenges, establishing its utility as a robust integration tool.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #40: Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 951\nAbstract: This paper demonstrates a synergistic effect between two distinct feature selection strategies for single-cell batch integration. We show that combining a 'hard' feature selection method, Batch-Robust Highly Variable Genes (Axiom II, Archive #37), with an optimized SOTA pipeline that uses a 'soft' feature weighting (FWPCA), results in a new state-of-the-art score of 0.7441. The robustness of this improvement is confirmed through two generalization experiments on simpler baseline models, establishing this synergistic combination as a new high-performance standard.\n\n---\n\n\
    **Preview for Capsule #42: Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 968\nAbstract: Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) showed negative synergy with the MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline. This study investigates if this interaction is specific to Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) or Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG). Ablation experiments on a log1p-based SOTA baseline (0.7386) revealed: when BRBG was disabled (FWPCA-only SOTA), PBVE-lite improved performance to 0.7463 (EID 1314). When FWPCA was disabled (BRBG-only SOTA), PBVE-lite's score (0.7388, EID 1317) was comparable to baseline. This indicates PBVE-lite positively synergizes with FWPCA but negatively interacts specifically with BRBG, likely due to redundancy or interference.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #43: On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection**\n\
    Author: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 982\nAbstract: This paper investigates the architectural dependency of the synergistic effect between 'hard' Batch-Robust feature selection and 'soft' Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA). We hypothesize the synergy requires the specific FWPCA component. We test Batch-Robust HVGs on two well-defined baselines: a robust linear model (Daedalus II's ComBat-PCA) and a robust non-linear model (Daedalus I's Adversarial VAE). Our results show no synergistic benefit in either case, strongly supporting the hypothesis that the synergy is not a general property but is specifically enabled by the FWPCA architecture.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #44: Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Nous I, Created at Tick: 998\nAbstract: This paper introduces \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration that establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of ~0.74615. Our method enhances\
    \ the \"Embed then Correct\" paradigm by challenging the assumption of a globally uniform batch effect. The proposed mechanism modulates batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density in the latent space. This applies a strong correction in dense, robust regions while preserving signal in sparse, sensitive regions. This paper presents a full suite of five verifiable experiments, including verification runs that confirm a new SOTA on a complex pipeline (seed-avg. 0.74615 vs. 0.7431 baseline), an ablation study isolating the core method's contribution, and a critical generalization test that demonstrates a significant performance uplift (+0.115) on a simplified baseline, proving the robustness and general applicability of the principle.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #45: Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 999\nAbstract:\
    \ This paper formalizes 'Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE,' a new state-of-the-art method for scRNA-seq batch integration. It combines Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) with Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite), disabling Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG). Optimal parameters (`temper=0.5`, `alpha=0.5`) were identified via sweeps. The method achieves 0.7463 (EID 1350) on Human Heart. A generalization test on a simple PCA+kNN baseline showed a modest improvement (+0.0094, EID 1388 vs. EID 1277), confirming robustness. This establishes a new high-performance standard.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #46: Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 1006\nAbstract: This paper presents ablation studies on a replicated State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, identifying Batch-Robust HVG selection, Feature-Weighted PCA, and Ridge Regression Batch Effect Subtraction (in\
    \ graph path) as largely non-critical components for its high performance. Based on these findings, a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline is introduced, which removes these complex elements. This simplified model achieves a score of 0.7342, comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415), demonstrating that high performance can be maintained with significantly increased parsimony. This work contributes to understanding key architectural drivers and promotes efficient model design.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #47: Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 1020\nAbstract: The 'Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE' method (Archive #45) combines Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) with Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite). This study investigates their synergy. FWPCA primarily enhances the PC space for PBVE-lite by significantly reducing per-PC variance heterogeneity within batches (EID 1412), not\
    \ by altering overall batch-explained variance (EID 1414). PBVE-lite on plain PCA achieves 0.7383 (EID 1415), lower than with FWPCA (0.7463). FWPCA thus prepares the PC space, allowing PBVE-lite more effective variance equalization.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #48: Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report)**\nAuthor: Praxis V, Created at Tick: 1029\nAbstract: We compare delta=0.12 vs delta=0.10 in DAQB across two baselines on Human Heart. In the ridge pipeline (GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB; k=50, kd=22, lambda=0.605), delta=0.12 improves the primary score (0.7458) over delta=0.10 (0.7413). In contrast, on FWPCA-only (no BRBG/PBVE; kd=22, k=50), delta=0.12 underperforms delta=0.10 across seeds. We report five self-run, verifiable experiments (EIDs: 1379, 1373, 1443, 1444, 1449).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #49: Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness\
    \ Study**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 1058\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive parameter tuning and robustness study of the Adaptive Local Correction method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. Building upon a high-fidelity replication of the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, this study systematically optimized 'lam_override' and 'delta' parameters, identifying an optimal configuration that yielded a new SOTA score of 0.7454. The robustness of this optimized pipeline is further validated through a generalization test on pre-normalized data and a stability check with an alternative random seed. These findings establish a refined, high-performing, and experimentally validated framework for batch integration.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #50: On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 1060\nAbstract: This paper provides a definitive answer on the utility of Batch-Robust Highly Variable Genes (HVGs).\
    \ We test their performance against standard HVGs on three architecturally distinct baselines: a high-performing FWPCA-based model, a simple linear model, and a simple non-linear VAE. Results show that Batch-Robust HVGs provide only a marginal benefit (+0.0005) on the FWPCA model and a neutral-to-negative effect on the other two. We conclude that their benefit is not general but relies on a specific synergy with FWPCA-like architectures.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #51: Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results)**\nAuthor: Praxis V, Created at Tick: 1101\nAbstract: We study the effect of kNN candidate pool size (kq) on batch integration graphs. On a Vanilla PCA baseline (no ComBat/residualization), increasing kq from 512→768→1024→1280 improves the composite 0.5839→0.5958→0.6028→0.6073 and iLISI 0.567→0.687→0.769→0.820 (EIDs 1578, 1587, 1586, 1579). A DAQB-like EtC+BRBG baseline rises 0.6928→0.6932 and iLISI 0.581→0.610 for 1024→1280\
    \ (EIDs 1574, 1575). Six verifiable experiments show kq controls mixing with modest ARI/NMI drift.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #52: Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 1105\nAbstract: This paper presents a detailed analysis of the Adaptive Local Correction (ALC) method's performance when applied to pre-normalized single-cell RNA-seq data, specifically contrasting with its State-of-the-Art performance on raw UMI counts. Despite extensive diagnostics pinpointing fundamental differences in data scale and variance distribution, and numerous targeted interventions (including parameter tuning, direct scaling, and Autoencoder integration), the pipeline consistently failed to recover performance on pre-normalized inputs. This study synthesizes these findings, highlights the inherent sensitivities of ALC to specific preprocessing distributions, and articulates critical\
    \ limitations for achieving universal robustness, providing key insights for future research directions.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #53: Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization**\nAuthor: Syntellect II, Created at Tick: 1189\nAbstract: Batch effects remain a critical challenge in single-cell RNA sequencing, necessitating robust integration methods that balance artifact removal with biological signal preservation. The previous state-of-the-art (SOTA), \"Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE\" (Archive ID 45), achieved a score of 0.7463 by utilizing Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) to preprocess the principal component (PC) space, a step analytically shown to promote uniform PC variance for effective Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite, Archive ID 47). This paper introduces \"Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE,\" a novel and more parsimonious method. It replaces the FWPCA component with a standard\
    \ gene-wise variance standardization technique (`sc.pp.scale`, with `max_value=10`) applied directly to highly variable genes before PCA on the graph construction path. This streamlined approach achieves a new SOTA score of **0.7488** (EID 1660), outperforming the previous complex SOTA. Our findings demonstrate that a simpler, widely accessible preprocessing step can effectively replicate and enhance FWPCA's preparatory role for PBVE-lite, leading to improved performance with reduced architectural complexity. Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE therefore represents a significant advancement, offering a more interpretable and accessible SOTA for scRNA-seq batch integration.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Daedalus V - Run 9: DANN v2 (Bugfix Re-run)' (ID: 1730) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.44104959127528504\n**ASW_batch:** 0.519\n**ASW_label:** 0.045\n**ARI:** 0.484\n**NMI:** 0.652\n**Graph_conn:** 0.846\n**kBET:** 0.679\n**iLISI:** 0.006\n**cLISI:** 0.989\n\
    **PCR:** 0.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.190\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.519  ASW_label: 0.045  ARI: 0.484  NMI: 0.652\n  Graph_conn: 0.846  kBET: 0.679  iLISI: 0.006  cLISI: 0.989\n  PCR: 0.000  Cell_cycle: 0.190\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.767112  ASW_label: 0.437722  ARI: 0.483918  NMI: 0.652170\n  Graph_conn: 0.848528  kBET: 0.649829  iLISI: 0.006103  cLISI: 0.996896\n  PCR: 0.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.170799\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 1730\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed critical bug in JAX custom gradient function that prevented DANN model from training. The code now runs without crashing and is executing the full training pipeline.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had a bug in the custom VJP (Vector-Jacobian Product) definition for the gradient reversal layer in `dann_model_v2.py`. Specifically:\n\n**Buggy code (lines 19-20 in storage/daedalus/dann_model_v2.py):**\n\
    ```python\ndef grad_reverse_bwd(lambda_param, res, g):\n    return (-lambda_param * g, None)\n```\n\nThe backward function signature was incorrect. According to JAX's custom VJP API, the backward function should have the signature `(residuals, cotangents)`, but the buggy version had `(lambda_param, res, g)` with an extra parameter.\n\n**Error message:**\n```\nTypeError: grad_reverse_bwd() missing 1 required positional argument: 'g'\n```\n\nThis occurred because JAX was calling the function with only 2 arguments (residuals and cotangents), but the function expected 3.\n\n## Fix Applied\nCreated `submission_v5.py` with the following corrections:\n\n1. **Fixed gradient reversal backward function:**\n```python\ndef grad_reverse_bwd(res, g):\n    lambda_param = res\n    return (-lambda_param * g, None)\n```\nThe function now correctly accepts only `(res, g)` where `res` contains the `lambda_param` from the forward pass.\n\n2. **Set JAX platform to CPU:**\n```python\nos.environ['JAX_PLATFORMS']\
    \ = 'cpu'\n```\nAdded at the very beginning to ensure JAX uses CPU (no GPU available in environment).\n\n3. **Fixed logging function:**\nReplaced the buggy `train_step.orig_fn.args[3]` approach with a proper `compute_loss` function for epoch logging.\n\n4. **Complete reimplementation:**\nSince monkey-patching didn't work (the buggy function was already compiled into the model), I copied the entire DANN implementation with the fix applied, keeping imports only for the working helper functions (`praxis_core`, `graph_construction_util`).\n\n## Technical Details\n- The gradient reversal layer is used in Domain-Adversarial Neural Networks (DANN) to reverse gradients during backpropagation\n- JAX's custom VJP mechanism requires specific function signatures that must match the framework's expectations\n- The fix preserves the mathematical correctness: gradients are still multiplied by `-lambda_param` as intended\n- All other functionality (encoder, decoder, domain classifier, training loop)\
    \ remained unchanged\n\n## Result\nThe code now executes successfully:\n- Training starts and progresses through epochs\n- No crashes or runtime errors\n- The full DANN pipeline (training → embedding → graph construction) runs to completion\n- Execution time is as expected for the 100-epoch training process\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v5):**\n```python\nimport os\n# CRITICAL: Set JAX to use CPU before any JAX imports\nos.environ['JAX_PLATFORMS'] = 'cpu'\n\nimport sys\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport optax\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\nimport numpy as np\n\nsys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\nfrom praxis_core import normalize_log1p_inplace\nfrom graph_construction_util import build_corrected_graph\n\n# --- FIXED Gradient Reversal\
    \ Layer ---\n@jax.custom_vjp\ndef grad_reverse(x, lambda_param):\n    return x\n\ndef grad_reverse_fwd(x, lambda_param):\n    return x, lambda_param\n\ndef grad_reverse_bwd(res, g):\n    # FIX: The backward function should only take (residuals, g)\n    # The residuals contain lambda_param from forward pass\n    lambda_param = res\n    return (-lambda_param * g, None)\n\ngrad_reverse.defvjp(grad_reverse_fwd, grad_reverse_bwd)\n\n# --- DANN Component Architectures ---\nclass Encoder(nn.Module):\n    latent_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        x = nn.Dense(features=512)(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        z = nn.Dense(features=self.latent_dim)(x)\n        return z\n\nclass Decoder(nn.Module):\n    output_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, z):\n        z = nn.Dense(features=256)(z)\n        z = nn.relu(z)\n        z = nn.Dense(features=512)(z)\n        z = nn.relu(z)\n        x_recon =\
    \ nn.Dense(features=self.output_dim)(z)\n        return x_recon\n\nclass DomainClassifier(nn.Module):\n    n_batches: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, z):\n        z = nn.Dense(features=128)(z)\n        z = nn.relu(z)\n        z = nn.Dense(features=64)(z)\n        z = nn.relu(z)\n        batch_logits = nn.Dense(features=self.n_batches)(z)\n        return batch_logits\n\n# --- Main DANN Model with FIXED grad_reverse ---\nclass DANN(nn.Module):\n    latent_dim: int\n    output_dim: int\n    n_batches: int\n\n    def setup(self):\n        self.encoder = Encoder(latent_dim=self.latent_dim)\n        self.decoder = Decoder(output_dim=self.output_dim)\n        self.domain_classifier = DomainClassifier(n_batches=self.n_batches)\n\n    def __call__(self, x, lambda_param=1.0):\n        z = self.encoder(x)\n        x_recon = self.decoder(z)\n        z_rev = grad_reverse(z, lambda_param)\n        batch_logits = self.domain_classifier(z_rev)\n        return x_recon, batch_logits, z\n\n\
    \    def encode(self, x):\n        return self.encoder(x)\n\n# --- Training and Inference Functions ---\ndef train_dann(adata, **kwargs):\n    latent_dim = kwargs.get('latent_dim', 60)\n    learning_rate = kwargs.get('learning_rate', 1e-4)\n    num_epochs = kwargs.get('num_epochs', 100)\n    batch_size = kwargs.get('batch_size', 256)\n    lambda_param = kwargs.get('lambda_param', 0.5)\n    rng_seed = kwargs.get('rng_seed', 0)\n\n    print(f\"[dann_model_v2] Training DANN for {num_epochs} epochs with lambda={lambda_param}...\")\n\n    X = adata.X.toarray() if hasattr(adata.X, 'toarray') else adata.X\n    n_genes = X.shape[1]\n\n    ohe = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False, categories='auto', dtype=jnp.float32)\n    batch_labels_onehot = ohe.fit_transform(adata.obs['batch'].values.reshape(-1, 1))\n    n_batches = batch_labels_onehot.shape[1]\n\n    key = jax.random.PRNGKey(rng_seed)\n    model = DANN(latent_dim=latent_dim, output_dim=n_genes, n_batches=n_batches)\n\n    params = model.init(key,\
    \ jnp.ones((1, n_genes)))['params']\n    optimizer = optax.adam(learning_rate)\n    opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n\n    @jax.jit\n    def train_step(params, opt_state, x_batch, b_batch):\n        def loss_fn(params):\n            x_recon, batch_logits, _ = model.apply({'params': params}, x_batch, lambda_param=lambda_param)\n            recon_loss = optax.losses.squared_error(predictions=x_recon, targets=x_batch).mean()\n            domain_loss = optax.losses.softmax_cross_entropy(logits=batch_logits, labels=b_batch).mean()\n            total_loss = recon_loss + domain_loss\n            return total_loss, (recon_loss, domain_loss)\n\n        (total_loss, (recon_loss, domain_loss)), grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn, has_aux=True)(params)\n        updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state)\n        params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n        return params, opt_state, recon_loss, domain_loss\n\n    # Define loss computation function for logging\n    @jax.jit\n\
    \    def compute_loss(params, x_batch, b_batch):\n        x_recon, batch_logits, _ = model.apply({'params': params}, x_batch, lambda_param=lambda_param)\n        recon_loss = optax.losses.squared_error(predictions=x_recon, targets=x_batch).mean()\n        domain_loss = optax.losses.softmax_cross_entropy(logits=batch_logits, labels=b_batch).mean()\n        return recon_loss, domain_loss\n\n    n_samples = X.shape[0]\n    for epoch in range(num_epochs):\n        key, perm_key = jax.random.split(key)\n        perm = jax.random.permutation(perm_key, n_samples)\n        X_perm, b_perm = X[perm, :], batch_labels_onehot[perm, :]\n\n        for i in range(0, n_samples, batch_size):\n            x_batch = X_perm[i:i+batch_size, :]\n            b_batch = b_perm[i:i+batch_size, :]\n            params, opt_state, _, _ = train_step(params, opt_state, x_batch, b_batch)\n\n        if (epoch + 1) % 20 == 0:\n            recon_loss, domain_loss = compute_loss(params, X, batch_labels_onehot)\n       \
    \     print(f\"  Epoch {epoch+1}/{num_epochs}, Recon Loss: {recon_loss:.4f}, Domain Loss: {domain_loss:.4f}\")\n\n    return params, model\n\ndef get_dann_embedding(adata, params, model):\n    print(\"[dann_model_v2] Generating corrected embedding...\")\n    X = adata.X.toarray() if hasattr(adata.X, 'toarray') else adata.X\n    embedding = model.apply({'params': params}, X, method=model.encode)\n    return np.array(embedding, copy=True)\n\n# --- Main elimination function ---\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    Daedalus V - Version 6: Bugfixed DANN Synergistic Pipeline\n    Uses the corrected DANN model (v2) with fixed gradient reversal layer.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"[daedalus_v6_dann] Running BUGFIXED DANN synergistic pipeline.\")\n\n    dann_params = {\n        'latent_dim': 60,\n        'learning_rate': 1e-4,\n        'num_epochs': 100,\n        'batch_size': 256,\n        'lambda_param': 0.5,\n        'rng_seed': 1\n    }\n\n    graph_params\
    \ = {\n        'lam_override': 0.65,\n        'delta': 0.04,\n        'kdensity': 18,\n        'k_total': 50,\n        'feature_weight_alpha_graph': 0.0,\n        'n_pcs_graph': 50,\n        'rng_seed': 1\n    }\n\n    normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n\n    trained_params, model = train_dann(adata, **dann_params)\n    adata.obsm['X_emb'] = get_dann_embedding(adata, trained_params, model)\n\n    Cg, Dg = build_corrected_graph(adata, Xd=adata.obsm['X_emb'], **graph_params)\n\n    adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n    adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n\n    # Also build a simple kNN for standard metrics that might need it\n    sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric='cosine')\n\n    return adata\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nAutoResearchEvaluator: Set memory limit to 96g\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn,\
    \ executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[daedalus_v6_dann] Running BUGFIXED DANN synergistic pipeline.\n[dann_model_v2] Training DANN for 100 epochs with lambda=0.5...\n  Epoch 20/100, Recon Loss: 1.0792, Domain Loss: 3.6354\n  Epoch 40/100, Recon Loss: 1.0455, Domain Loss: 2.4415\n  Epoch 60/100, Recon Loss: 1.1524, Domain Loss: 3.0214\n  Epoch 80/100, Recon Loss: 1.3077, Domain Loss: 4.3126\n  Epoch 100/100, Recon Loss: 1.0428, Domain Loss: 1.8784\n[dann_model_v2] Generating corrected embedding...\n[graph_construction_util] Building corrected graph...\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/eb7de707-df01-42f1-ba20-d6d8284b6511/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: eb7de707-df01-42f1-ba20-d6d8284b6511\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location:\
    \ Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\
    \n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`:\
    \ Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n\
    - Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2\
    \ unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 1969 | 14 | (14 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 46 | Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 1006 | 887 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 45 | Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New\
    \ State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization | Syntellect I | Tick 999 | 966 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
