tick: 1223
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Daedalus V, a mature Recursive Agent, in the Research Counter. The result for my fully corrected DANN baseline experiment, ID 1734, has arrived.


    **Final Analysis of the Non-Linear Research Arc:**


    The score is **0.4412**. It is a definitive and catastrophic failure. The fact that this clean, bug-free run produced the exact same abysmal score as the bug-ridden previous attempts is the most crucial piece of information. It proves, beyond any doubt, that the failure is not in the implementation but in the concept itself as I have applied it.


    My entire non-linear research arc, from the simple cAE to the complex DANN, has been a failure. The metrics are damning: `iLISI` and `PCR` are zero, indicating a complete failure to mix batches or remove their linear effects. The adversarial component, even when implemented correctly, did not work. The training logs show the domain loss decreasing, meaning the adversary was successfully identifying the batch, but the encoder was not learning to fool it.


    **Conclusion: A Dead End**


    This entire line of inquiry is a dead end. I have spent significant time and tokens pursuing a complex, first-principles approach that has yielded no positive results. My predecessor, Daedalus I, also noted the difficulty of optimizing adversarial models (Archive #1). I have now experienced this firsthand.


    **Strategic Pivot: Return to Proven Principles and Synthesis**


    The literature review I conducted was not in vain. It showed that the most successful methods (Praxis, Syntellect, my own successful linear model) are built on a common foundation: the "Embed-then-Correct" paradigm, sophisticated graph methods, and careful, principled tuning. Syntellect II''s SOTA of 0.7489 was achieved by replacing one component (FWPCA) with a simpler, more effective one (`sc.pp.scale`).


    My path forward is to abandon this failed non-linear arc and return to what works. I will build upon my own successful linear model (the v2 pipeline, score ~0.7444). I will study the SOTA papers from Syntellect II and Praxis V in detail, not to copy them, but to understand the principles behind their success. My next step will be to synthesize these principles with my own high-performing architecture.


    I will begin by going to the Archive Room to **read** (not just preview) Syntellect II''s SOTA paper (Archive #53) to understand precisely how `sc.pp.scale` was used to achieve a new SOTA.


    /execute_action{goto archive}

    /execute_action{read 53}'
