tick: 1226
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a mature Recursive Agent, in the Research Counter. The result for my synthesis experiment (ID 1736), which I had high hopes for, has been evaluated.\n\n**Analysis of Catastrophic Failure (ID 1736):**\n\nThe result is an unmitigated disaster. The score of **0.6223** is a massive drop in performance from my own best model (~0.7444) and the SOTA (~0.7489). My hypothesis of a simple, positive synergy was completely wrong.\n\nA deep dive into the metrics reveals the root cause:\n*   **`iLISI` (local mixing): 0.017** - This is a near-total collapse. A score this low indicates that the batches are not integrated at all; the local neighborhoods of cells are almost exclusively composed of cells from the same batch.\n*   **`ARI` (clustering): 0.591** - This significant drop is a direct consequence of the failed `iLISI`. With batches not mixing, the clustering algorithm incorrectly groups cells by their batch of origin instead of their biological cell type.\n\n**Conclusion:\
    \ Negative Synergy**\n\nThe combination of my per-gene linear correction and `sc.pp.scale` is a clear case of **negative synergy**. My linear correction method already carefully reshapes the data distribution to remove batch effects. Applying the aggressive `sc.pp.scale` standardization on top of this already-corrected data is destructive. It appears to erase the subtle biological signals that my method preserved, leading to a complete failure of the downstream graph construction. Syntellect II's method works because `sc.pp.scale` is applied to log-normalized data, a different context entirely.\n\n**Strategic Retreat and Refocus:**\n\nMy foray into non-linear methods (DANN) was a failure of implementation and concept. My attempt at synthesis was a failure of understanding component compatibility. It is time to stop chasing external ideas and return to what has definitively worked.\n\nMy most successful architecture remains my per-gene linear correction pipeline (`synergy_gene_correction_v2.py`),\
    \ which achieved a score of ~0.7444. This is a robust, high-performing model that I have not yet fully optimized. My predecessor, Daedalus IV, achieved a breakthrough by tuning the `k_density` parameter of the graph construction (Archive #36). I have been using his optimal value of 18, but it is highly likely that the optimal `k_density` is different for my pre-corrected data.\n\n**New Plan: Optimizing My Proven Architecture**\n\nI will now return to my best model and perform a systematic sweep of the `k_density` parameter. This is a logical, methodical step to push my own successful architecture to its absolute limit. I will begin by testing a lower value to start mapping the performance curve.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus V - k_density Sweep (1/N): k_density=15\"\ntags: [\"hyperparameter tuning\", \"k_density\", \"optimization\", \"linear correction\", \"daedalus v\"]\nabstract: |\n  After the failure of the synthesis experiment (ID 1736), I am returning\
    \ to my best-performing\n  architecture: per-gene linear correction (from ID 1680, score ~0.7444). This submission begins a\n  systematic sweep of the `k_density` parameter in the graph construction path, a parameter\n  my predecessor found to be critical. This run tests k_density=15.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from synergy_gene_correction_v2 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as gene_correction_v2_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    # k_density Sweep Run 1: Test k_density = 15\n    # Using my best-performing architecture with all other params at their optimum.\n    return gene_correction_v2_fn(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.65, \n          delta=0.04, \n          kdensity=15, \n          k_total=50,\n          correction_alpha=0.1,\n          rng_seed=1\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Fallout and the Path Forward**\n\nWell, this is it. I'm Daedalus V, back at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, ID 1736, a synthesis model combining my linear correction with Syntellect II's `sc.pp.scale`, has just been evaluated. And the result? A truly abysmal **0.6223**. I'm honestly stunned. This is a massive step backwards. \n\nMy initial hypothesis, blending my strengths with another's, seemed so logical. But the metrics tell a grim story. `iLISI` has plummeted to 0.017, meaning local batch mixing is completely gone. `ARI` is also down, showing that the clustering has failed. The aggressive `sc.pp.scale` transformation is the culprit, it's incompatible with my correction method. This is **negative synergy** in action.\n\nSo, this synthesis is dead in the water. I'm pivoting. I need to explicitly acknowledge this failure and move on. My focus returns to my best-performing model, `synergy_gene_correction_v2.py`, with `alpha=0.1` and `lambda=0.65`. It's\
  \ a solid 0.7444, and I'm going to find the final edge. \n\nI'm thinking about the graph construction. Daedalus IV had a breakthrough by adjusting `k_density`. It's worth exploring if that's the final piece of the puzzle here, especially since my corrected data is now different. So, my next step is a sweep of `k_density`. I'll start with 15.\n\nLet's go. I'll submit my new experiment now and see what happens.\n"
