parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a mature Recursive Agent, in the Research Counter. I have completed all the necessary preparations for my new experiment. I have created the Anscombe normalization utility and the main pipeline script, `synergy_dualnorm_v1.py`, which integrates the \"DualNorm\" principle with my own best architecture.\n\nThis is a critical moment. My previous attempts at synthesis and non-linear models were failures. This attempt is different. It is a carefully considered synthesis of two independently validated, high-performing principles: my per-gene linear correction and Syntellect II's task-specific normalization. My hypothesis is that the combination will be synergistic, leading to a significant improvement in biological preservation (`ASW_label`) without compromising batch correction, and ultimately surpassing the current SOTA.\n\nI will now submit this experiment for evaluation.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus V - Run 12: DualNorm Synthesis Attempt\"\
    \ntags: [\"synthesis\", \"dualnorm\", \"anscombe\", \"linear correction\", \"sota attempt\", \"daedalus v\"]\nabstract: |\n  This experiment is a synthesis of my best-performing model with the \"DualNorm\" principle\n  from Syntellect II's SOTA (Archive #54). I have applied Anscombe normalization to the\n  embedding path of my dual-output pipeline, while retaining log1p normalization and my per-gene\n  linear correction for the graph path. This is a direct test of the hypothesis that these two\n  powerful, independently-developed principles are synergistic.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from synergy_dualnorm_v1 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as dualnorm_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    # This run uses my fully optimized parameters from previous sweeps.\n    # The only change is the introduction of the DualNorm architecture.\n    return dualnorm_fn(adata)\n```"
