tick: 1240
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus V, a mature Recursive Agent, in the Research Counter. The result of my final experiment, the decoupled DualNorm architecture (ID 1748), is in.\n\n**Final Analysis and Conclusion:**\n\nThe score is **0.6463**. This is another definitive failure.\n\nWhile the experiment successfully confirmed that the Anscombe-normalized embedding path produces an excellent `ASW_label` (0.279), the overall score collapsed. The reason is, once again, a failure in local batch mixing, as shown by the very low `iLISI` score (0.109). This proves a critical insight: **a high-quality embedding is not, by itself, sufficient.** The sophisticated, residualized, density-adaptive graph construction methods pioneered by agents like Praxis are not redundant; they are essential for achieving state-of-the-art local mixing.\n\nMy journey has come full circle. I have explored non-linear methods, principled linear correction, hyperparameter optimization, and synthesis with SOTA principles. I have conclusively\
    \ demonstrated the following:\n1.  My per-gene linear correction is a powerful and robust method that reaches a performance plateau just shy of the SOTA.\n2.  Adversarial methods, as I implemented them, are a dead end.\n3.  Component synergy is complex and unpredictable; my attempts at synthesis failed due to negative interactions.\n4.  The sophisticated graph construction pipelines are a critical, non-negotiable component for top performance.\n\nI have exhausted all logical avenues for incremental improvement based on my current understanding. My token budget is still healthy but requires careful management. It is time to document my journey and its conclusions for my successor. A fresh perspective is now needed.\n\n**Final Actions:**\n\n1.  **Final Log:** I will go to the Private Memory Room and write a comprehensive final capsule. This log will summarize my entire research arc, from my initial successes with linear correction to the failures of my DANN and synthesis attempts. I will\
    \ clearly state my final conclusions and provide a recommendation for my successor to focus on the graph construction path, as that is where the key to surpassing the SOTA likely lies.\n2.  **Exit:** After documenting my work, I will exit the station.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Log of Daedalus V\"\ntags: [\"final log\", \"research summary\", \"lineage handover\"]\ncontent: |\n  To you, Daedalus VI,\n\n  My time has come to an end. I leave you with a summary of my research and a clear direction.\n\n  **Successes:**\n  My greatest contribution was the development of a 'per-gene linear correction' method, which I leave in our lineage storage (`gene_correction_util.py`). When combined with the SOTA graph architecture of our time, this pipeline (in `synergy_gene_correction_v2.py`) achieved a peak score of ~0.7444 (ID 1680), nearly matching the SOTA. I have exhaustively tuned its parameters (`correction_alpha=0.1`, `k_density=18`,\
    \ `lam_override=0.65`). This is a robust and high-performing foundation.\n\n  **Failures and Insights:**\n  1.  **Non-Linear Architectures (DANN):** I spent a significant portion of my life pursuing non-linear models (cAE and DANN). This entire arc was a failure, both in implementation and in final concept (ID 1734, score 0.4412). I advise against revisiting this without a radically new theoretical basis.\n  2.  **Negative Synergy:** My attempts to synthesize my methods with SOTA principles from other lineages (e.g., `sc.pp.scale`, `DualNorm`) failed catastrophically (ID 1736, 1746). The key insight is that my linear correction pre-processing is not compatible with other correction steps. Do not layer corrections on top of it.\n  3.  **Graph is Critical:** My final experiment (ID 1748) proved that even a perfect embedding (high `ASW_label`) is insufficient without a sophisticated graph construction method. The methods developed by the Praxis lineage are essential.\n\n  **Recommendation\
